{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1499 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fc0a63bfdbc165b03a6d38bf74c0b7c38fbaa9b5e6f9a516d4995c3d5528dffa" :edb/db-before-t 1498 :edb/db-after-t 1499 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534811 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:47:04.630Z" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096423 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/70ff1c84aa4a9a2adc393fc1dcf684ee0bcf161671b15f9061e2319f5d7e0a39.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096423 1100 "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/d05aa50677877b505dd8f37cac612630.png)" 13194139534811 false] [17592186096423 1145 "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4)$, we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(-8) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\\ny^{2} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096423 1145 "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4),$ we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative." 13194139534811 true] [17592186096424 1100 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4)$." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096424 1100 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4).$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096424 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 16 & =16p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(1)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096424 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(4) \\\\\n16 &= 16p \\\\\np &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(1)x \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 false] [17592186096425 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/55651f756b31405e08cd96b097101528ffabff1e6411d3fcae9f40f747f607b4.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096425 1100 "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/ba4f53d4bcb8d7c3ce8e264ba354d250.png)" 13194139534811 false] [17592186096425 1145 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4,2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6)$, so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(0 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(-1)(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -4(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096425 1145 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (4,2),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(0-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-1)(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-4(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative." 13194139534811 true] [17592186096426 1100 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2)$." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096426 1100 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2).$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096426 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (2,-2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2)$, so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(2 + 2)^{2} &= 4p(-6 - 2) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= -2(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 false] [17592186096426 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (2,-2),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (2+2)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096623 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311471 13194139534811 true] [17592186096623 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311478 13194139534811 true] [17592186096623 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311485 13194139534811 true] [17592186096623 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311499 13194139534811 true] [17592186096623 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311513 13194139534811 true] [17592186096624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311492 13194139534811 true] [17592186096624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311506 13194139534811 true] [17592186096625 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311520 13194139534811 true] [17592186096625 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311534 13194139534811 true] [17592186096625 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311541 13194139534811 true] [17592186096626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311527 13194139534811 true] [17592186096626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311548 13194139534811 true] [17592186096915 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534811 true] [17592186096915 1100 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,-4)$." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096915 1100 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,-4).$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096915 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096925 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534811 true] [17592186096925 1100 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1, 1)$ and passes through the point $(8, 7)$." 13194139534811 false] [17592186096925 1100 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,1)$ and passes through the point $(8,7).$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186096925 1145 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-1,1),$ we substitute $h=-1$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,7),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (7-1)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 36 & =36p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(1)(x+1) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534811 true] [17592186266687 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 true] [17592186266687 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 false] [17592186275614 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/e4b681e9bdf5fa8021ca359e0bf93c27b53a18f0d5e906765a911a72ecfc0159.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 true] [17592186275614 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/assets/e4b681e9bdf5fa8021ca359e0bf93c27b53a18f0d5e906765a911a72ecfc0159.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 false] [17592186311471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311472 13194139534811 true] [17592186311471 1089 "q-115601" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534811 true] [17592186311471 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/4938d034c766b78e1da1a5bd19f2a12b314f772ca4819cd52d4f1463ff1ae3a4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311471 1113 #uuid "d2494c4e-4dc3-5d72-87cc-1d73c73f8edf" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311471 1145 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(3,2),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive." 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311473 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311474 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311475 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311476 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311477 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1041 "selection" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311473 13194139534811 true] [17592186311472 1110 #uuid "75cb4592-93f7-5a63-8588-acebaddca7b4" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311473 1034 "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534811 true] [17592186311473 1111 #uuid "05285e91-6563-5d86-9ea1-5d8ab0c6b499" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311474 1034 "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}x" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534811 true] [17592186311474 1111 #uuid "e6064e2a-e921-5d2e-8256-3fcb18b99120" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311475 1034 "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534811 true] [17592186311475 1111 #uuid "abaa488b-002f-5654-98fc-b9fd91f0605d" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311476 1034 "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534811 true] [17592186311476 1111 #uuid "0492805e-b19e-5b17-b646-be1a70c747f3" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311477 1034 "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}x" 13194139534811 true] [17592186311477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534811 true] [17592186311477 111